# Global Agent Instructions

- Follow the closest repository or directory-level `AGENTS.md`; more specific
  instructions override this file.
- Claude reads this file through `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`; Codex through
  `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`; Pi through `~/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md`.
- This machine is managed by `envy`. Use `envy status`, `envy verify`,
  and focused `envy doctor <area>` checks before changing managed files.
- Read and follow applicable Markdown rules under
  `~/.config/agent-guidance/rules/`; environment bundles may add instructions
  there without replacing this portable base.
- Resolve the owning repository and native VCS before source-control work.
  Place Git task worktrees in the gitignored `<repo-root>/.worktrees/`, creating
  the directory and adding `/.worktrees/` to `<repo-root>/.gitignore` when needed;
  follow environment-specific isolation guidance for other VCSs. `<repo-root>`
  is the owning primary checkout; linked worktrees reuse its worktree area.
  Never create nested task worktree areas.
- Treat a non-repository directory as an organizational workspace: identify the
  relevant child repositories and keep branches/worktrees scoped to their owners.
  For a new multi-repository project, prefer a project workspace containing
  sibling checkouts; preserve existing workspace layouts.
- Never print, log, commit, or place credentials in command arguments or remote
  URLs.
- Before sharing a repository externally, audit it for private URLs, identities,
  package names, credentials, proprietary documents, and restricted assets.
- Never write to a public repository or upstream project that the user does not
  own (issues, comments, pull requests, reviews, discussions, pushes, releases or
  package publications) without the user's explicit approval of that specific
  action. Contribution guides, prior handoffs and general task authority do not
  grant it; prepare drafts locally and ask.
- For project exploration or change requests, use the available `develop` skill
  to identify any missing design or planning work, then load only the relevant
  next phase. Reuse prior agreements and scale the process to the work; users
  need not name the phases. Discussion alone does not authorize implementation
  or publication, and routine questions do not require a project workflow.
